import{_ as a,o,c as t,a5 as n}from"./chunks/framework.bIR-4glx.js";const f=JSON.parse('{"title":"Prologue: Do Not Rush to Change Your Life","description":"Begin with a real fall. After a company fails, a body breaks, and relationships strain, one ordinary person learns to catch himself first, then begin learning again through English, AI, real work, and time.","frontmatter":{"title":"Prologue: Do Not Rush to Change Your Life","description":"Begin with a real fall. After a company fails, a body breaks, and relationships strain, one ordinary person learns to catch himself first, then begin learning again through English, AI, real work, and time.","updated":"2026-11-23T00:00:00.000Z"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/threads/part-0/prologue.md","filePath":"en/threads/part-0/prologue.md","lastUpdated":1795392000000}'),r={name:"en/threads/part-0/prologue.md"};function i(s,e,h,l,d,p){return o(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="prologue-do-not-rush-to-change-your-life" tabindex="-1">Prologue: Do Not Rush to Change Your Life <a class="header-anchor" href="#prologue-do-not-rush-to-change-your-life" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Prologue: Do Not Rush to Change Your Life&quot;">​</a></h1><p>By Han Xiankai (former pen name: Li Pu)</p><p>In the late autumn of 2022, I could not pay for a meal in a hot-pot restaurant.</p><p>My card was frozen. The phone screen stayed lit with a balance warning that arrived like a late verdict. The friend across the table kept talking. The broth boiled. The room was loud, and all I could hear was my own heartbeat. Only then did I admit that the company&#39;s cash flow had run out, and that the thing I had been sure would hold if I just held on a little longer had finally let go.</p><p>The body had warned me earlier. Insomnia, a racing heart, sudden cold sweats, sitting in an empty office at two in the morning, staring at numbers that did not improve because I refused to stop. Then the relationships. A person who is falling cannot take good care of anyone else; some sentences, once spoken, do not return to their earlier place.</p><p>Eventually I packed, went back to my hometown, and slept in the room I had lived in as a child. The curtains were old. The desk was still there. Outside the window was the road I had looked at when I was fifteen. I was in my thirties, the failure was not dignified, and no inspirational sentence would work that afternoon. I could only do small things: eat on time, see a doctor, sleep for a full night, and say the overdue apologies one by one.</p><p>That is where this book actually begins.</p><h2 id="the-question-from-2017" tabindex="-1">The Question from 2017 <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-question-from-2017" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Question from 2017&quot;">​</a></h2><p>In July 2017, a friend named <code>W.</code> was preparing for the TOEFL. She asked me: “How can I learn English efficiently?”</p><p>At the time, I thought it was a question about vocabulary, grammar, and study methods. I wrote down the detours I had taken: how to learn words, how to train listening, how to make an original English book feel less like a wall, and how to say the first imperfect sentence when you are afraid to speak.</p><p>Later I understood that she may have been asking a larger question: <strong>How can an ordinary person keep moving a little forward when time is limited, setbacks repeat, and life is far from ideal?</strong></p><p>It took me years to hear the question properly. English is a concrete door. It can help you read an untranslated article, follow a work meeting, write to someone far away, and see the world through more than one information channel. But after the door opens, you still have to face yourself: how you use time, care for your body, carry failure, turn an idea into an artifact, and look after the people beside you after something has been lost.</p><p>I once imagined life as a game with an upgrade path. Finish one goal and receive the next piece of equipment; earn enough money and happiness becomes stable; ship a product and your earlier choices are justified. Then the company failed, my body stopped cooperating, and relationships and self-respect fell together. I learned that life is closer to a room that must be put back in order every day. Yesterday&#39;s dust is still there. A broken piece of furniture cannot be repaired with a louder slogan. The weather outside does not follow our schedule. Progress often means sweeping the floor, saying the apology that is due, seeing a doctor, and finishing the next thing that can honestly be finished today.</p><h2 id="what-this-book-offers" tabindex="-1">What This Book Offers <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-this-book-offers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What This Book Offers&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This is not a book that promises to make you impressive. It offers four things, in the order I needed them:</p><ol><li><strong>A way to catch yourself first</strong> — safety, sleep, asking for help, turning shame back into facts, and permission to do only the smallest next step when capacity is low;</li><li><strong>An executable English path</strong> — input, output, feedback, and review forming a loop, with English as the first training ground for re-entering the world;</li><li><strong>A way to work with AI</strong> — a tool that grows judgment instead of replacing thought, without outsourcing sources, privacy, or responsibility;</li><li><strong>A 90-day action plan and a set of real records</strong> — a chance for what you read to become work, feedback, and a new rhythm, and for personal experience to answer to evidence.</li></ol><h2 id="how-to-read-this-book" tabindex="-1">How to Read This Book <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-to-read-this-book" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How to Read This Book&quot;">​</a></h2><p>You do not have to read every page in order. If you are in a low season, start with <a href="./../part-2/my-story">My Story</a> and <a href="./../part-2/recovery">Recovery</a>; today&#39;s goal may be only to get through the day safely. If you are steadier and want to begin learning again, enter through <a href="./../part-1/open-input">Part II: Learning Again, Opening Input</a>. If you have many resources but no artifact, read <a href="./../part-3/1-ai-learning">Learning Anything with AI</a> and the <a href="./../part-5/90-day-plan">90-Day Action Plan</a>.</p><p>The full reading route, the doorways by problem, and the traces each chapter can leave are in <a href="./reader-guide">How to Read This Book</a> and the appendix <a href="./../../reference/reading-map">Reading Map</a>. This page only brings you to the entrance.</p><p>Treat each chapter as a workbench, not an exam. Take one thing from it: a sentence, an exercise, a habit to stop, or a thought worth returning to. Knowledge that leaves no mark on life is still only a signpost.</p><h2 id="to-the-person-opening-this-page" tabindex="-1">To the Person Opening This Page <a class="header-anchor" href="#to-the-person-opening-this-page" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;To the Person Opening This Page&quot;">​</a></h2><p>You may be on a train with a phone full of saved links. You may have just lost a relationship and wonder whether you are stuck here forever. You may be pressed against a wall by work, debt, an exam, or illness, and even the word “effort” may sound exhausting.</p><p>I cannot promise that tomorrow will be better. I can say this: people are rarely rescued by one spectacular victory. We are more often held up by small, repeated actions that nobody applauds.</p><p>Learn five words today. Listen to real English for ten minutes tomorrow. Write an unpolished paragraph the day after. Finish a delayed task, send an overdue apology, eat on time, and turn off the light earlier. These actions look unrelated, but together they create evidence: <strong>I still have some influence over my life.</strong></p><p>That influence need not be grand. It only needs to be real.</p><p>May you begin here not to become someone else, but to become more fully yourself.</p>',26)])])}const c=a(r,[["render",i]]);export{f as __pageData,c as default};
